✅ Can We Save .jmx Files in GitLab?
✔️ Yes — it’s best practice to:
Store your JMeter test plans (.jmx) in GitLab
Use branches/tags for versioning
Collaborate via merge requests
Run tests via GitLab CI/CD pipelines
🧱 Recommended Repository Structure
Here’s a clean and scalable structure you can use:
jmeter-performance-tests/
├── README.md                         <-- Project usage guide
├── jmeter-tests/
│   ├── login-flow.jmx                <-- Test for login
│   ├── search-flow.jmx               <-- Test for search
│   ├── full-user-journey.jmx         <-- End-to-end test
├── data/
│   ├── users.csv                     <-- Test data files
│   ├── search-terms.csv
├── results/
│   ├── latest-results.jtl           <-- Result logs (optional)
│   └── HTMLReport/                   <-- Auto-generated report (optional)
├── scripts/
│   ├── run-jmeter.sh                <-- Shell script to run JMeter
├── .gitlab-ci.yml                   <-- GitLab pipeline config
└── config/
    └── test-env.properties           <-- Custom variables
🧑‍💻 Simple User Guide for Teams (Short & Shareable)
🔹 1. Clone the Repo
git clone https://gitlab.com/your-org/jmeter-performance-tests.git
cd jmeter-performance-tests
🔹 2. Open or Modify a Test Plan
jmeter -t jmeter-tests/login-flow.jmx
Or open via GUI:
jmeter
→ File > Open > jmeter-tests/login-flow.jmx
🔹 3. Run a Test from Terminal
jmeter -n -t jmeter-tests/login-flow.jmx -l results/results.jtl -e -o results/HTMLReport
🔹 4. Review the HTML Report
Open results/HTMLReport/index.html in a browser.
🔹 5. Push Changes
git checkout -b feature/update-login-test
git add .
git commit -m "Updated login test with correlation"
git push origin feature/update-login-test
Create a merge request for review.
💡 Pro Tips
Task	Recommendation
Test Data	Store in /data/, use CSV Data Set Config
Result Artifacts	Don't commit .jtl or /HTMLReport/ — add to .gitignore
Parameterization	Use variables via user.properties or /config/
Collaboration	Use merge requests with review comments
